# kext-vtable-padding: the kexts this kernel loads come from a Mac kernelcache, whose classes carry
# OSMetaClassDeclareReservedUnused() slots. XNU emits those only for XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX, and this
# kernel builds as PLATFORM=iPhoneOS, so every virtual method after the first padding block sits at
# a lower vtable index here than in the kext's headers. AppleImage4 then calls
# obj->vtable[0x2d8] expecting IORegistryEntry::getProperty and lands on IOService::handleOpen,
# whose signing salt differs, so the authenticated call fails:
#   "PAC failure from kernel with IA key while branching to X8" at pc 0xfffffff009581910.
# Measured on 25F84: 296 of 298 kernel classes order their virtuals identically to Apple's, 150 are
# index-identical once the padding is dropped and 146 differ only by insertions.
# Only correct while the loaded collection is a Mac collection: an iPad collection expects padding
# off, so this patch belongs to the macOS profiles and must not be shared with an iOS series.
kext-vtable-padding.patch

# fileset-wait-probe: a kext waiting for a service this kernel never publishes produces no output at
# all (waitForService(matching, NULL) becomes UINT64_MAX). AppleImage4 blocks exactly that way after
# its nonce magazine reports "failed to read nonce slot data", because it waits for "IONVRAM" and
# "IOAESAccelerator" and neither exists in this collection. Inert unless the boot args are passed:
#   fileset-wait-log=1        print each request (provider class + timeout) before the wait
#   fileset-wait-cap-ms=N     cap an unbounded wait at N ms so the caller takes its own error path
fileset-wait-probe.patch

# publish-ionvram-resource: AppleImage4 waits with no timeout for the "IONVRAM" resource
# (resourceMatching("IONVRAM") = IOResources + IOResourceMatch). Apple publishes that resource from
# IOPlatformExpert::registerNVRAMController() when a platform NVRAM controller registers; this kernel
# creates its IODTNVRAM from the device tree's /options node but never registers a controller, so the
# resource never appears and the wait never returns. Publishing it where the IODTNVRAM instance is
# registered claims exactly what is true and lets the kext continue.
publish-ionvram-resource.patch

# lck-gate-probe: B14 still stopped with the same 12 nonce-magazine lines and printed no
# fileset-wait: line at all, so AppleImage4 never reached a waitForService. In that function
# (0x958922c) the calls right after the magazine read are IOLockLock / lck_mtx_gate_close /
# IOLockUnlock, and both a stuck mutex and gate_ilock() (a spin on the gate's interlock bit with no
# timeout) hang silently. This prints at the entry of every lck_mtx_gate_* call and on every
# contended lck_mtx_lock, so one boot says which of the two it is.
lck-gate-probe.patch

# uke-heartbeat: a stuck kext and a console that stopped refreshing look identical on the panel,
# which is the ambiguity B12..B15 kept hitting (AppleImage4 prints twelve nonce-magazine lines and
# then nothing changes). Two seconds apart, a thread call prints "uke-heartbeat <n>": if those keep
# appearing the kernel still runs and only one thread is blocked, and if they stop the console itself
# stopped updating.
uke-heartbeat.patch

# uke-stage-last-panic: B22's panel showed the gate probe firing (lck-gate: alloc_init) and then the
# twelve AppleImage4 nonce-magazine lines, and after that not one uke-heartbeat line: the whole kernel
# or at least the console path stopped, which is exactly why the deferred panic-flush and ramoops
# patches existed. Those need UkeLastLog's staging block and a consumer, so this is the self-contained
# version: the panic funnel formats its text into the pram panic header that check_for_panic_log()
# already copies at boot, and the enabled last-panic-print patch shows it once the framebuffer console
# attaches. A panic that never reached the screen therefore reports itself on the next boot.
uke-stage-last-panic.patch

# uke-gate-last-panic: B23 staged the panic text in the pram header and last-panic-print() shows it
# from vcattach(), but the two panels taken after that boot start at AppleImage4's own banner: the
# line had already scrolled off. The gate probe does print where the panel still updates, so it now
# carries the staged text on its first call, immediately before the point where the screen stops.
# "(no previous panic captured)" means the last boot did not panic at all — a spin, not a panic.
uke-gate-last-panic.patch

# uke-spin-watchdog: B25's panel shows "PREVIOUS XNU LOG (no previous panic captured)" next to the
# gate probe, so the freeze is not a panic at all — it is a wait that never ends, and it takes the
# console with it. hw_spin_should_keep_spinning() is where a "no timeout" policy spins forever, so a
# lock that stays contended for millions of passes now panics with the lock address and the caller,
# and that panic text is staged in pram (uke-stage-last-panic) so the next boot prints it.
# The gate probe line also reports the pram region, because "(no previous panic captured)" only means
# something if the staging area is real.
uke-spin-watchdog.patch
# The timer interrupt samples the interrupted PC, so a hang with interrupts enabled (a
# driver polling a register that never changes, a stub returning nonsense) or one with them
# masked (a spin, caught by another CPU going quiet) panics with the address instead of
# freezing the panel. Every threshold is a boot arg, so tuning needs no rebuild.
uke-stall-probe.patch

# The virtual timer's PPI 27 is enabled in the GIC from the boot CPU and from every secondary, because
# on this platform nothing else ever did: pe_fiq.c (the only code that does it) is compiled for VMAPPLE
# only, so the timer counted down and no interrupt was ever forwarded. Group 0 (FIQ) when the
# distributor is single-security-state, group 1 (IRQ) otherwise, in which case sleh_irq dispatches it.
# uke-gic=0 turns the whole thing off.
# uke-gic-timer.patch is retired: ArmCpu (drivers/ArmCpu) is the component that arms the GIC
# and the architectural timer and registers the CPU IRQ action, so the hand-rolled GIC bring-up
# duplicated it and stole its interrupt. The probe below reports ArmCpu's own state instead.

# From the gate probe on, the sampler reports samples/last_pc/cpus and arms the stall detector. ArmCpu's own
# flags cannot be printed from the kernel: ArmCpu is a kext the emitter links after the kernel, so its symbols
# do not exist at kernel link time (that was a link error). Whether the platform layer came up is read from
# behaviour instead - samples > 0 means the architectural timer is delivering.
uke-armcpu-probe.patch

# AppleImage4's start waits without a timeout for a class IOAESAccelerator service; the class is prelinked but
# no instance exists. uke-aes-stub=1 publishes an instance of the original class at the wait itself (no vtable
# binding needed), and the wait probe moved onto waitForMatchingServiceWithToken, the implementation every path
# funnels through, because the wrapper produced no output at all.
uke-aes-stub.patch
